elaine: Quick heads-up — trimming the Next starter to reduce onboarding friction. Running into inconsistent auth/retry behavior between the browser and server SDK builds. Short thread with repro and a proposed fix.

mike: thanks! can you drop a minimal repro and SDK version?

elaine: using @redwood/sdk-js v0.12.1 (browser bundle) + Next API route. repro pattern: token refresh race when a request is in-flight and the refresh triggers -> duplicate refresh calls, some requests 401 after backoff.

sam: ugh. any console / network traces?

elaine: pasted a minimal snippet below — the problem is the auth layer starts a refresh but doesn't await the shared promise before replaying requests.
```js
// simplified
const api = new RedwoodClient({ auth: { refresh: refreshToken } });
// user code
await api.generate({ prompt: 'hi' });

// inside sdk-auth
if (this._isRefreshing) {
  // missing await here
  this._startRefresh(); // starts a refresh but we continue
}
// later we re-send original request immediately
```

mike: looks like the browser build's fetch wrapper doesn't await the refresh promise. adding `await this._refreshPromise` should prevent the double refresh.

elaine: i tried that locally — it stops dup refresh calls and the retry backoff behaves. but CI/SSR tests fail: the server build deadlocks in SSR case where refresh expects cookies that aren't available.

ci-bot: CI / build #421 failed on main — unit test "auth:browser refresh concurrency" failed (expected 1 call). tail: AssertionError: expected 1 to equal 0

sophia: if awaiting causes SSR deadlock, maybe the concurrency guard should be browser-only. server builds can use stateless refresh flow. we should detect runtime: browser vs node/edge.

john: +1. ensure SDK parity by having two codepaths: browser (await shared refresh promise + client-side backoff) and server (stateless retry). add feature flag env REDWOOD_SDK_BROWSER_CONCURRENCY to opt-in.

mike: plan: 1) implement platform guard + env override, 2) add e2e in starter app (quickplay sample) that simulates token refresh with msw and asserts single refresh call, 3) update docs/upgrade notes. also add test matrix for node/edge/browser.

elaine: i'll open a PR with the guard and tests, plus a small sample tweak in the starter to set REDWOOD_SDK_AUTO_BACKOFF=1 in the dev env. putting notes in the quickstart README so new users don't hit this. thanks all :thumbsup:

sam: ping me on the PR, happy to help with the msw harness.

mike: nice — please also add a short changelog entry describing behavioral difference between browser/server auth flows so platform teams are aware.

elaine: will do. leaving this thread for context and the starter repo issue link when PR is up.
